+def transcript_verdict(text, tail_lines=10):
+    """Return "pass" only for an unambiguous canonical PASS marker at the end.
+
+    Three conditions, all fail-closed:
+      * at least one `VERDICT:` line exists;
+      * EVERY such line is byte-for-byte "VERDICT: PASS" after trimming — no
+        "PASS WITH RISKS", no lowercase, no "APPROVE";
+      * the last one is among the final `tail_lines` non-empty lines, so it is
+        the reviewer's closing word rather than something near the top.
+
+    The whole transcript is read, not a tail window: a review that says FAIL and
+    then emits 5 KB of appendix would otherwise have its failure scroll out of
+    view. The closing-position rule is the other half of the same idea — a
+    prompt echo containing the marker, followed by a review that never reaches a
+    verdict, must not count as approval. The small allowance exists because real
+    reviewers append a footer (Codex prints "tokens used" and a count after its
+    reply); it is not room for review content.
