commit 5cce1a778b9b5cd1be741449635b052ae04469ec
Author:     Daniel Siman Tov <[REDACTED:email]>
AuthorDate: Fri Sep 4 10:55:19 2026 +0300
Commit:     Daniel Siman Tov <[REDACTED:email]>
CommitDate: Fri Sep 4 10:55:19 2026 +0300

    defer item 2; harden the reviewer sandbox; stop the suite spending review budget
    
    Five cold review rounds, 26 verified findings, and one mechanism accounted for a
    fail-open in every single round: carrying forward the human-gate obligation that
    "verification is not authorization" requires. It was shed by stacking a commit
    on top (round 1), under --human-gate-scope irreversible (round 2), by an L0
    successor and by scope going retroactively sticky (round 3), through --base
    (round 4), and finally by carrying the approval while dropping the reviewer
    requirement (round 5). Each fix created the next finding.
    
    So it is DEFERRED, not patched again. Removed: _pending_elevation,
    pending_human, inherited_human_gate, the EVIDENCE_VERIFICATION/AUTHORIZATION
    scopes and the ancestor-scope logic. _validated_baseline is back to the released
    semantics — a baseline advances only under the full authorization requirements.
    The fleet's inflated floors stay, which is over-strictness: the safe direction.
    docs/GATE.md records the cost and the five-round history.
    
    Round-5 findings closed in what does ship:
    
    - The reviewer packet now covers the SAME span the floor is computed over
      (baseline..HEAD, or base...HEAD), not just `git show HEAD` — otherwise an
      unreviewed migration followed by a harmless commit derived L3 while both
      reviewers saw only the harmless commit, and their transcripts were recorded as
      evidence for the whole span.
    - The outside-worktree sandbox no longer inherits repo-reachable search paths:
      PYTHONPATH/PYTHONHOME/PYTHONSTARTUP dropped, and every PATH entry that is
      empty (= cwd), relative, or inside the repo filtered out. Changing the cwd
      alone still let `python3 -m reviewer` import a planted reviewer.py.
    - A git failure while producing the diff stops the run instead of handing the
      reviewer an empty packet to pass.
    - Scratch dirs are removed on every path, including each STOP.
    
    And the reason GLM failed closed in four of five rounds: the suite drives the
    real reviewer CLIs with a stubbed transport — nothing is sent — but log_egress
    still wrote `attempt` markers to the operator's PRODUCTION egress log, and the
    daily spend cap counts exactly those. A full run burned ~30 slots. The suite now
    runs against a temporary log, restores it instead of unsetting it, and
    tearDownModule FAILS if the production log is touched at all. Verified by
    measurement: 2673 lines before and after a full run.
    
    264 tests, all green.

