    87	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    88	# Deterministic risk classification (the tier FLOOR, derived from the change
    89	# itself).
    90	#
    91	# Why this exists: every gate below keys off `risk_tier`, but that tier was
    92	# self-declared at `init`. A change mislabelled L1 silently skips the L3 gates —
    93	# no alarm, no evidence that anything was skipped. This is the one place the
    94	# whole chain could be defeated by a typo or by optimism. So the tier a human
    95	# (or an AI) declares is now only a FLOOR CANDIDATE: `classify` reads the paths
    96	# that actually changed and emits its own floor, and `gate --min-tier` takes the
    97	# MAX. Classification can therefore only ever RAISE the requirement.
    98	#
    99	# Rules are PATH-based on purpose: paths are cheap, stable, and reviewable, and
